WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
01 / SELECT A DUE-DATE RANGE
Choose UTC due dates. The end boundary is exclusive.
Default range: 1-8 October 2026. Every manufacturing record is invented.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:20.000
02 / FIND THE FOUR DELAYED ORDERS
Show = Delayed narrows the nine-order cohort to four work orders.
One completed late; three were still open at the cutoff. Two review cases stay separate.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:30.000
03 / INSPECT THE COMPLETION EVIDENCE
WO-1002 completed after its due time. Both copies of EV-02 remain visible.
The identical replay is collapsed for classification, without changing the source rows.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:40.000
04 / CATCH THE 26-VERSUS-9 JOIN ERROR
The independent reference confirms nine orders. The deliberate bad join counts 26 rows.
Validation catches 17 extra rows and 1,195 units instead of the correct 365.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:50.000
05 / READ THE QUERY THAT FAILS
Joining raw process steps and completion events multiplies one-to-many relationships.
COUNT(*) then counts joined rows instead of work orders. This query is intentionally wrong.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:00.000
06 / GROUP BEFORE JOINING
The corrected SQL reduces events and steps before joining one row per work order.
Validated dates are bound separately. A procedural Python reference checks the result.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:10.000
07 / SAVE A SNAPSHOT TO SQLITE
Save snapshot stores the report results, evidence, parameters and SQL text together.
The dataset fingerprint is checked again during the save transaction.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:20.000
08 / REOPEN THE PRESERVED RESULT
After interrupting and restarting the app with the same data directory, open the snapshot.
The interface identifies the frozen result as SAVED SNAPSHOT. A snapshot is not a backup.
